==Status Report: 2026-10-24 (Friday)==
Implemented two things that address the reported "media card disappears after a
minute or two when paused" symptom plus the settings entry to stay alive in the
background:

1. Settings → "Background playback" → "Battery optimization". A tappable row
   (BatterySaver icon, subtitle reflects the current exemption state) that fires
   `ACTION_REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS` directly (system popup), with
   fallbacks to `ACTION_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATION_SETTINGS` then
   `ACTION_BATTERY_SAVER_SETTINGS`. Added `REQUEST_IGNORE_BATTERY_OPTIMIZATIONS`
   to the manifest. State is re-read on resume via `LifecycleResumeEffect` so
   the subtitle updates when the user returns from the dialog.

2. Kept the playback service foreground while paused. Media3 only requests the
   foreground while actively playing; when paused it posted a plain notification
   and let the service out of the foreground, so Doze/LMK could kill the process
   and take the media card with it. `WcastMediaService.onUpdateNotification` now
   forces `startInForegroundRequired` while the session still has a timeline
   (playing OR paused), releasing it only when the stream finishes (empty
   timeline). This is what keeps the drawer card alive across a long pause.

Open question still to verify on-device: whether an OEM "aggressive" battery
saver can still sweep a foreground+paused media session even with the exemption;
the battery-optimization row is the user-facing lever for that.

